deep.reconstruction:
Context, from the previous page: Σ is the round unit sphere in Euclidean 3-space E. Three points R_j (j ∈ J, |J| = 3) lie on the equator and form an equilateral triangle, so they sum to zero. This gives a decomposition E ≅ V_J(R) ⊕ Δ, where:
- V_J(R) = Ker(R^J → R) is the equatorial plane, carrying the unique S_J-invariant quadratic form with q(R_j) = 1 (x²−xy+y² in a basis of two of the R_j);
- Δ = V^⊥ ≅ R(ε) is the polar axis.

Formula (8) gives the action of g ∈ S_J on E. It acts tautologically, by permuting coordinates, on V_J(R), and by the sign sg(g) on Δ. A transposition is a reflection on V and −1 on Δ, so its determinant is +1: it is the rotation by π about the axis through the fixed vertex. So (8) realizes S_3 as the dihedral rotation group D_3 ⊂ SO(3), acting on the sphere by orientation-preserving (holomorphic) maps that permute the three points.

Formula (9) adds τ = id_V ⊕ (−id_Δ), the reflection in the equatorial plane. τ is orientation-reversing and fixes the equator, so it is complex conjugation in a model where the R_j are real points of P^1 (e.g. 0, 1, ∞). The full group S_3 × {1, τ} (order 12, D_3h) is therefore the automorphism group of (P^1_C, {0, 1, ∞}) including antiholomorphic maps. This is the topological shadow of S_3 × Gal(C/R).

Formula (10) sets Σ* = Σ ∖ {R_i}, a topological model of P^1 − {0, 1, ∞}. Grothendieck announces he will study the fundamental groupoid of Σ*, with the action of this symmetry group, 'as a preliminary exercise in Galois synthesis'. Galois synthesis means reconstructing groupoids with their symmetry and Galois actions out of symmetric local pieces.

He then lists generalizations:
- other symmetric configurations on the sphere (implicitly the Platonic/polyhedral ones);
- other surfaces;
- the sphere replaced by the Hopf fibration;
- applications to computing Teichmüller groupoids.

Finally he proposes compactifying Σ* to obtain base points 'close to' the R_i. On the next page this becomes the real oriented blow-up, whose boundary circles are the unit tangent circles at the R_i. These are tangential base points.
